# Cyrius Field Notes — Language Gotchas (stdlib & file format)
# str/cstr boundary surprises, toml + cyml format quirks, the sandhi
# service-layer stdlib dep, and the `dist/<pkg>.deps` sidecar.
# Refreshed 2026-08-13 at v6.5.21: vendored sandhi re-stamped 1.9.9 → 1.9.10
# (closes a P1 where response framing returned SANDHI_OK carrying a NULL
# body pointer on an OOM body copy — reachable on ordinary arena-backed
# exchanges, not just under memory pressure).
# Prior refresh 2026-08-07 at v6.5.10: vendored sandhi re-stamped 1.9.3 → 1.9.9
# (verified against `lib/sandhi.cyr`'s own header, not against a doc), the
# `[deps] stdlib` example lost `"json"` and `"toml"` — neither `lib/json.cyr`
# nor `lib/toml.cyr` exists, both were carved into bayan at v6.1.25 — and the
# `.deps` sidecar entry was added from v6.5.10.
# Prior refresh 2026-07-27 at v6.4.82: `str_data_not_nul_terminated` now
# states the literal → Str coercion RULE (it fires only for a param
# annotated `: Str`), and the two entries that were missing their
# `category = "cyrius"` line got it.
# 6 entries.
# v6.7.11: distlib_deps_sidecar_is_a_machine_check_not_a_readme gained the owner rule's tie refusal (`WS_*`) and the
# program-wide fold global (`_bre_err`). still 6 entries (DERIVED at 6.7.11 with a line-anchored grep — the two `[[entries]]` lines in
# cyml_toml_plus_markdown_frontmatter's example are indented, not entries).
# v6.7.12: +1 entry — a_const_and_its_public_var_twin_are_safe_only_when_lib_reads_the_const_v6712; the distlib sidecar
# entry gained the end of the `WS_*` tie (D11: a companion module includes its sibling's constants). 7 entries (DERIVED).
# v6.7.14: +1 entry — a_tls_peer_that_pads_or_repeats_now_fails_the_handshake_v6714 (LIB-8). Updated:
# a_const_and_its_public_var_twin_are_safe_only_when_lib_reads_the_const_v6712 (`TLS_SHA256_CTX_LEN` has its twin and is
# inert — 15 names, LIB-2). 8 entries (DERIVED at 6.7.14 with the line-anchored grep).
# v6.7.15 (2026-10-19): +1 entry — a_corrupt_patra_value_reads_patra_err_page_not_an_empty_ok_v6715 (patra 1.16.5: LF-2,
# LF-3, LF-7). Updated: a_tls_peer_that_pads_or_repeats_now_fails_the_handshake_v6714 (the 1.2 ServerHello and the
# ClientHellos a native server reads fill their message, D40, PL-5, PL-6). 9 entries (DERIVED at 6.7.15 with the
# line-anchored grep; cymlcheck.py's stripped match still counts the example's two indented lines, so it reports 2).
# v6.7.16 (2026-10-21): no new entry. Updated: a_tls_peer_that_pads_or_repeats_now_fails_the_handshake_v6714 (the
# native servers read the whole ClientHello — the 1.2 server's compression / extension framing, D32, PL-9; a partial
# entry, a repeated type, compression other than null, D81 – D83, PP-7 – PP-9; CYRIUS-2026-0037 recorded),
# a_corrupt_patra_value_reads_patra_err_page_not_an_empty_ok_v6715 (patra 1.16.6: a negative length refused at the
# write and read as -1 / PATRA_ERR_PAGE, D34, D35). 9 entries (DERIVED at 6.7.16 with the line-anchored grep;
# cymlcheck.py still reports the example's 2).

[[entries]]
name = "str_data_not_nul_terminated"
description = "str_data(s) returns a pointer into the Str's backing buffer which is NOT NUL-terminated"
category = "cyrius"
---
`Str` is a fat pointer `{data_ptr, len}`. `str_data(s)` returns
the raw data pointer. That buffer is NOT guaranteed to be
NUL-terminated — Str carries its length explicitly. Passing
`str_data(s)` to a cstr-consuming function (`streq`, `strlen`,
`sys_write(..., cstr_len)`) will walk off the end.

    # WRONG — streq uses strlen; walks past the Str's end
    streq(str_data(s), "literal");

    # RIGHT — use the Str-aware comparison
    str_eq_cstr(s, "literal");

    # RIGHT — use the length explicitly for byte ops
    sys_write(1, str_data(s), str_len(s));

The symmetric rule: cstr literals have an implicit trailing NUL.
Passing `"hello"` where a Str is expected is a type mismatch —
UNLESS the receiving parameter is annotated `: Str`, in which
case the compiler coerces the literal for you. That annotation
is the whole trigger:

    fn f(s: Str) { ... }   f("hello");   # coerced — a real Str
    fn g(s)      { ... }   g("hello");   # NOT coerced — raw cstr,
                                         # and str_len(s) then reads
                                         # load64(ptr+8) = garbage.
                                         # SILENT empty/zero result.

Wrapping the call site in `str_from("hello")` always works and is
what you want when the callee is out of your control. But if the
callee IS yours, annotate the param — see
`semantics_runtime.cyml::string_literal_to_str_coercion_is_annotation_only`
(the bug that hid in 11 `lib/fs.cyr` fns for ~a year, fixed v6.4.72).

CONVERSION TABLE:
    str_from(cstr)        → Str (copy ptr + compute length)
    str_data(s)           → cstr-shaped byte ptr (NOT NUL-term)
    str_len(s)            → length
    str_eq_cstr(s, c)     → Str vs cstr comparison
    str_eq(a, b)          → Str vs Str comparison
    streq(a, b)           → cstr vs cstr comparison
    memeq(p1, p2, n)      → raw n-byte memory compare

Surfaced v5.4.17 writing `tests/tcyr/toml_multiline.tcyr` — a
`streq(str_data(pair_key), "key_cstring")` check always returned
0 because `streq` read past the Str's data into whatever
garbage bytes followed. Fix: use `toml_get(pairs, "key_cstring")`
which does the right thing internally via `str_eq_cstr`.

[[entries]]
name = "toml_parse_strips_quotes"
description = "lib/bayan.cyr's _toml_parse_str returns the string WITHOUT surrounding double-quotes (TOML parsing consolidated into bayan; lib/toml.cyr no longer exists)"
category = "cyrius"
---
When `lib/bayan.cyr` parses a double-quoted TOML value like
`host = "example.com"`, the value returned by `toml_get` is
`example.com` — the quotes are stripped by `_toml_parse_str`.
(TOML parsing was consolidated into the bundled `lib/bayan.cyr`;
the old `lib/toml.cyr` no longer exists. `toml_get` is a thin
wrapper over `bayan_toml_get`.)
Test assertions must match the stripped form.

    # TOML source:
    # host = "example.com"

    var v = toml_get(pairs, "host");
    str_eq_cstr(v, "example.com");        # → 1 (correct)
    str_eq_cstr(v, "\"example.com\"");    # → 0 (don't include quotes)

Unquoted values (numbers, booleans) pass through verbatim:
    port = 8080      → "8080"
    enabled = true   → "true"

Array values are returned with their brackets preserved (the
caller walks the bracketed form to extract elements):
    commands = ["/a", "/b"]   → `["/a", "/b"]`

Triple-quoted strings (`'''multi-line'''`) also have their
triple-quote delimiters stripped; the body bytes (including any
internal newlines) come through as-is.

This matters for tests that assert on toml_get's output: always
write the EXPECTED value in its stripped form, not the on-disk
quoted form.

[[entries]]
name = "stdlib_str_to_cstr_key_migration"
description = "cyrius 5.x stdlib lookups (toml_get, toml_get_sections, …) now take cstr keys; passing str_from(\"…\") silently returns 0/nothing"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE CHANGE:

In older stdlib (cyrius 4.x toml.cyr era), keyed lookups accepted
Str arguments:

    toml_get(pairs, str_from("id"))            # used to work

The 5.x rewrite implemented these via `str_eq_cstr(key_in_pair, key_arg)`,
which expects the *second* argument to be a null-terminated C string:

    fn str_eq_cstr(s, cstr) {
        var slen = str_len(s);
        var clen = strlen(cstr);               # ← walks until NUL
        if (slen != clen) { return 0; }
        return memeq(str_data(s), cstr, slen);
    }

Pass a Str (no NUL terminator) and `strlen` reads past the buffer
into whatever follows in memory. The returned `clen` is some
random non-matching length, so the equality check fails and every
lookup returns 0.

This is silent: the program does not crash inside `toml_get`. It
crashes downstream — in vidya's case, when a 0 id flowed into
`path_join(0, fname)` and faulted at NULL.

CORRECT SHAPE (cyrius 5.7+):

    toml_get(pairs, "id")
    toml_get_sections(sections, "best_practices")

Plain string literals (cstrs in cyrius). Any place you wrapped a
key in `str_from(...)` for a stdlib lookup helper is wrong now.

GENERAL RULE:

When a stdlib function's parameter is named `cstr`, `name`, or
`key` and the implementation calls `strlen` on it, the argument
MUST be a null-terminated C string — string literals qualify,
`str_from(...)` results do not. Read the helper before you call
it; the type sigil is just `var`, so the contract is in the body.

WHERE THIS BIT US:

vidya 2.3.0 — every concept_id/title/topic/description/tags from
`concept.toml` came back as 0 after the 5.7.0 stdlib refresh.
Combined with the static-data buffer aliasing (see
`var_buf_in_library_functions`), the symptom presented as
"all topics share garbled fragments of the last file" — two
unrelated stdlib bugs stacking. The first lookup that returned
non-zero would have been a coincidence.

[[entries]]
name = "cyml_toml_plus_markdown_frontmatter"
description = "CYML = TOML header + markdown body, separated by `---`. The cyrius 5.x ecosystem moved manifests + content files to this format; `lib/bayan.cyr` parses it (parser consolidated into bayan; lib/cyml.cyr no longer exists)"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE FORMAT:

CYML is "TOML frontmatter + markdown body" — the same shape Hugo
and Jekyll use. Two layouts:

  Single-entry file:
    [package]
    name = "vidya"
    version = "${file:VERSION}"
    cyrius = "5.8.27"
    ---
    # vidya
    Programming reference library and queryable corpus.

  Multi-entry file:
    # file-level header (TOML, optional)

    [[entries]]
    name = "first"
    category = "..."
    ---
    body of the first entry, free-form markdown.

    [[entries]]
    name = "second"
    ---
    body of the second entry.

The delimiter is a bare `---` on its own line. Inside an entry,
everything before `---` is parsed as TOML; everything after is
treated as opaque text (markdown by convention).

PARSER:

`include "lib/bayan.cyr"` provides (the parser was consolidated
into the bundled `lib/bayan.cyr`; `lib/cyml.cyr` no longer
exists — `cyml_parse` is a thin wrapper over `bayan_cyml_parse`):

  cyml_parse(data, len) → CymlDoc                  (zero-copy)
  cyml_doc_entry_count(doc)
  cyml_doc_entry(doc, n) → CymlEntry
  cyml_entry_header(e), cyml_entry_header_len(e)   (TOML bytes)
  cyml_entry_body(e),   cyml_entry_body_len(e)     (markdown bytes)

Pointers returned are aliases into the input buffer; keep the
buffer alive for as long as you use the entries (heap-alloc it,
not `var buf[N]` — see `var_buf_in_library_functions`).

WHERE THIS LANDED:

  cyrius.cyml             — every project manifest (5.5+)
  vidya field-notes       — content/cyrius/**/*.cyml (post-2.3.0
                            migration from `*.toml`)
  sandhi/sakshi/patra docs — same convention

CYML vs TOML:

CYML is a superset by convention only — the parser doesn't know
how to read a TOML file with a triple-quoted `content = '''...'''`
block. The migration drops the `content = '''...'''` wrapper and
moves the body below `---` instead, with one `[[entries]]` block
per logical entry.

FRONTMATTER GOTCHA:

Plain `---` lines inside the markdown body terminate the body.
If your prose needs literal `---` (e.g. quoting a CYML example),
indent it or fence it inside a code block — the parser only
matches `---` at column 0.

[[entries]]
name = "sandhi_service_layer_dep"
description = "sandhi (सन्धि — junction) is the cyrius service-boundary stdlib: HTTP client/server, TLS, service discovery. Ships with the toolchain (vendored at 1.10.8 since cyrius 6.6.19; the live fold version is in dependencies.cyml `existing_deps_registry`) — declare as `\"sandhi\"` in `[deps] stdlib = [...]`"
category = "cyrius"
---
WHAT IT IS:

sandhi composes the thin network primitives in stdlib (`http.cyr`,
`net.cyr`, `tls.cyr`, `ws.cyr`, plus `bayan.cyr` for JSON) into the
full client + server patterns AGNOS consumers actually need. Same architectural
shape as sakshi (logging primitives → tracing layer) and mabda
(GPU primitives → render foundation): a curated layer above the
raw kernel-syscall stdlib.

API SHAPE:

  HTTP server:
    sandhi_server_run(addr, port, handler_fp, ctx)
    sandhi_server_get_path(buf, blen)
    sandhi_server_get_param(path, name)
    sandhi_server_path_segment(path, n)
    sandhi_server_send_response(cfd, code, msg, ct, body, blen, hdrs)
    sandhi_server_send_chunked_start / _send_chunk / _send_chunked_end
    HTTP_OK / HTTP_NOT_FOUND / HTTP_BAD_REQUEST / INADDR_ANY()

  HTTP client:
    sandhi_conn_open(addr, port, use_tls, sni_host)
    sandhi_conn_send_all / _recv_all
    sandhi_url_parse(url) → SandhiUrl

  Headers + smuggling defenses:
    sandhi_headers_new / _add / _set / _get / _serialize / _parse
    sandhi_server_request_has_dup_smuggling_header
    sandhi_server_request_has_cl_te_conflict

DEPS WIRING:

sandhi is a stdlib module as of cyrius 5.7.0 — it folded in from
its sibling-crate scaffold and now ships with the toolchain. List
it by name in `[deps] stdlib = [...]`:

    [deps]
    stdlib = [
        "syscalls", "string", "alloc", "str", "fmt", "vec",
        "hashmap", "io", "fs", "tagged", "fnptr", "args",
        "regex", "net",
        "sandhi",
    ]

⚠ There is no `"json"` and no `"toml"` to list — `lib/json.cyr` and
`lib/toml.cyr` were carved into the **bayan** sibling at v6.1.25 and
neither file exists any more (verified 2026-08-07). The `json_*` /
`toml_*` names still work, as thin wrappers over `bayan_*` inside
`lib/bayan.cyr`; list `"bayan"` (or `include "lib/bayan.cyr"`
explicitly — bayan is opt-in, not auto-prepended) instead.

`cyrius deps` reads the manifest and copies the listed modules
from `~/.cyrius/lib/` into the project's `lib/` directory. Stdlib
modules are versioned by the cyrius toolchain (the `cyrius = "X.Y.Z"`
pin in `[package]`), not by per-module pins, so they don't appear in
`cyrius.lock` — only `[deps.<name>]` git stanzas (e.g. sakshi,
patra) get hashed.

Pattern rule: stdlib names go in the array, heavier external
git-pinned libraries go in `[deps.<name>]` stanzas. Don't mix
the two for the same module.

WHY THE LAYER EXISTS:

The thin primitives are kernel-safe — they call syscalls and
nothing else. The composed patterns (request smuggling defenses,
TLS connect-with-timeout, per-route handlers, chunked streaming)
require allocation, retries, header normalization, and other
"servicey" behaviors that don't belong in a kernel-consumable
stdlib. Splitting them keeps the stdlib bare-metal-shaped while
giving userland a batteries-included service layer.

WHERE THIS LANDED:

vidya 2.3.0 was the first downstream consumer to wire sandhi in,
replacing the project-vendored `lib/http_server.cyr` it had been
carrying since 2.2.0. The migration is mechanical: drop the
vendored include, swap the `http_*` callers to `sandhi_server_*`
(the constants `HTTP_OK` / `HTTP_NOT_FOUND` / etc. are re-exposed
by sandhi for source-compat), and add `"sandhi"` to the stdlib
list.

[[entries]]
name = "distlib_deps_sidecar_is_a_machine_check_not_a_readme"
description = "`dist/<pkg>.deps` is the file `cyrius deps` validates a CONSUMER's `[deps] stdlib` against — so an under-reporting sidecar does not merely mislead a reader, it switches the check OFF and the consumer builds green while under-declaring. Until v6.5.10 it was built only by scanning bundled sources for literal `include \"lib/X.cyr\"` lines, so it captured what a module INCLUDES and never what it REFERENCES through the declaration. Now it is the declared `[deps] stdlib` UNIONED with that scan — base bundle only; profiles keep the pruned inference on purpose."
category = "cyrius"
---
WHAT THE FILE IS. `cyrius distlib` emits `dist/<pkg>.deps` beside the
bundle: one stdlib leaf per line, `#` for comments. Its header says it
lists "stdlib leaves this fold requires in scope", and it is read by
machine — `cyrius deps` validates a consumer's `[deps] stdlib` array
against the dep's sidecar and hard-errors, naming the missing leaves.

THE DEFECT (filed from setu 0.8.2, fixed v6.5.10). It was built ONLY by
scanning the bundled module sources for literal `include "lib/X.cyr"`
lines. So it captured what a module **includes** and never what it
**references** through the manifest declaration. setu emitted **8**
leaves against a declared **12**; the four missing — `result`, `net`,
`chrono`, `args` — are all referenced at top level in `src/client.cyr`
with no `include` line of their own.

⭐ **THE PART THAT MAKES IT MORE THAN A DOC BUG.** Both directions were
measured before filing: with a corrected 12-leaf sidecar an
under-declaring consumer got a hard error naming `chrono` and `args`;
with the raw 8-leaf one the **same** consumer built `OK`, silently. A
wrong sidecar disables the machine check that exists to catch exactly
that mistake. If you ship a fold, its sidecar is load-bearing.

THE RULE NOW: the sidecar is the declared `[deps] stdlib` **unioned**
with the include-scan, never either alone. The scan can legitimately
catch a leaf the author forgot to declare, so the union can never
under-report relative to either source.

⚠ **BASE BUNDLE ONLY.** A `[lib.X]` profile is a narrow subset of
modules, so its true dep set is a subset of the declaration; unioning
the whole declaration into a `sha` profile would OVER-report and could
fail a legitimately-narrow consumer against the very check this
re-enables. Profiles keep the pruned inference. Do not "fix" that to
match the base bundle.

⚠ **REGENERATE EVERY PROFILE, NOT JUST THE BUNDLE.** `cyrius distlib`
used to regenerate exactly one bundle per invocation, which is how
sankoch 2.7.6's gzip fix nearly shipped with all nine sub-bundles still
carrying the buggy encoder under a fresh version string. Use
`cyrius distlib --all`; `--check` compares **bytes**, not version
strings, which is precisely what let those profiles look fresh.
(⛔ Before v6.5.8 both flags were swallowed by the arg loop and ran the
base-bundle-only path exiting 0 — the command the issue told people to
reach for already "succeeded" while doing the wrong thing.)

THREE HYPOTHESES ALREADY FALSIFIED, recorded so nobody re-investigates:
it is NOT the declaration (deleting `assert` from `[deps] stdlib` left
it in the sidecar), NOT an inline-comment parse bug (adding a comment to
`vec` left it present), and NOT a direct-symbol scan. It was simply a
different, smaller thing than its own header claimed.

# NOTE: the "adding public stdlib fns trips lint + api-surface + cyrdoc"
# process rule lives as a single canonical entry in
# compiler/methodology.cyml (stdlib_public_fn_addition_trips_three_hygiene_gates).

6.7.11 — `cyrius distlib` MAY REFUSE A TIE: INCLUDE THE LEAF. When two stdlib leaves declare a name a bundle uses and
neither is in its unit, distlib refuses by name instead of picking the first file (`'X' is undefined on every target,
and more than one stdlib leaf could own it (a, b) — include the right leaf from a bundled module. Sidecar NOT
written`); `include "lib/<leaf>.cyr"` from the module that uses it. The live case: `WS_*` (ws / ws_server). The same
census found a fold's top-level global shared program-wide with another fold's (`_bre_err`, sankoch / niyama — fixed
in sankoch 2.8.7): a fold's top-level names are program-wide, so a private one wants a fold-specific prefix.
language/tooling.cyml compiler_flags § `cyrius distlib` FLAGS (the owner rule). CHANGELOG [6.7.11] (TL-4).

6.7.12 — A COMPANION MODULE INCLUDES ITS SIBLING'S CONSTANTS; IT NEVER COPIES THEM (D11 — LIB-1). The `WS_*` tie is
gone: `lib/ws_server.cyr` now INCLUDES `lib/ws.cyr`, and the nine `WS_*` opcodes / states are ws.cyr's enum
constants (distlib records `ws`; the owner-rule gate's ALLOW list is empty). Two stdlib leaves declaring one name — even
with the same value — are a tie: since 6.7.11 distlib's one-owner rule refuses every bundle that reads that name with
neither leaf in its unit (yantra's distlib converges now). The cost of the include is ws.cyr's fns (DCE drops them)
and its enum constants, which cannot be assigned or have their address taken. CHANGELOG [6.7.12] LIB-1.

[[entries]]
name = "a_const_and_its_public_var_twin_are_safe_only_when_lib_reads_the_const_v6712"
description = "A `const _X` / `var X = _X;` pair is safe only when the library reads `_X` EVERYWHERE: one read of the public var lets a caller's write steer storage the const sized. 6.7.9 fixed the non-TLS twins; 6.7.12 fixed the TLS stack's 198 sites (`TLS12_RANDOM_LEN = 256` was a SIGSEGV in the PRF seed copy). The public names stay writable but inert (D16) — 15 since 6.7.14, when the literal `TLS_SHA256_CTX_LEN` gained its const twin."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.12", "v6.7.14", "const", "public var", "twin", "bound vars", "tls", "TLS_RECORD_MAX_PLAINTEXT", "TLS_SHA256_CTX_LEN", "D16", "D17"]
---
THE SHAPE. 6.7.6 gave each public bound a `const _X` that sizes its storage, and kept `var X = _X;` for the API. The
pair is safe only when the library reads `_X` everywhere: one read of the public var lets a caller's write steer
storage the const sized. 6.7.9 made the const drive every loop and allocation outside the TLS stack; `lib/tls_native*`
still read the vars until 6.7.12 — `TLS12_RANDOM_LEN = 256` copied 512 bytes into the 64-byte PRF seed (SIGSEGV), a
raised `TLS12_VERIFY_DATA_LEN` overran `expected`, a raised `TLS_SHA384_CTX_LEN` overran the transcript clone. 198
reads now go to `_X` (`lib_buffers_sized_by_const.sh` axis 6 holds every derived twin).

WHAT A CALLER SEES. The public names stay WRITABLE but INERT (D16): a write reads back (they are still vars) and
changes nothing — read-only mirrors. 14 at 6.7.12; 15 since 6.7.14. `TLS_RECORD_MAX_PLAINTEXT` is a protocol constant
(D17), and "tuning" it never worked: a lowered value refused peers' full 2^14-byte records.

A LITERAL PUBLIC VAR THAT SIZES A COPY INTO CONST-SIZED STORAGE IS THE SAME HAZARD as a twin read. `var
TLS_SHA256_CTX_LEN = 144;` had no twin through 6.7.13 and sized both the SHA-256 transcript's heap ctx and the copy into
its 208-byte stack clone: a program that set it to 4096 crashed inside `tls_native_transcript_digest` (139; an access
violation on Windows), and one that lowered it under-allocated the ctx. Since 6.7.14 it has the twin shape — `const
_TLS_SHA256_CTX_LEN = 144;` + `var TLS_SHA256_CTX_LEN = _TLS_SHA256_CTX_LEN;`, lib/ reads the const — so a write is
inert like the other fourteen (6.7.12's D16, a fifteenth name). language/stdlib_modules.cyml tls_module_contract_v6614
(BOUND VARS). CHANGELOG [6.7.12] LIB-11, LIB-12, LIB-13; [6.7.14] LIB-2.

[[entries]]
name = "a_tls_peer_that_pads_or_repeats_now_fails_the_handshake_v6714"
description = "Since 6.7.14 the native TLS client refuses what 6.7.12's conformance bite left: a server that repeats an extension, leaves bytes after an extension block, or answers ALPN with anything but exactly one non-empty name (two names: the client used to take the first) fails the handshake with an alert, and a 1.2 ServerHello is walked against the 1.2 ClientHello. Since 6.7.15 (D40) every extension block fills its message both ways: a 1.2 ServerHello, and a ClientHello a native SERVER reads, with bytes after the block, an overrunning block or a lone length byte fails with decode_error. Since 6.7.16 the native servers read the whole ClientHello: a missing, empty or overrunning compression_methods list, an extension that does not frame, a partial last entry, a repeated extension type or compression other than null fails with decode_error or illegal_parameter (D32, D81 – D83). That is the RFC's answer — the fix is on the peer."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.14", "v6.7.15", "v6.7.16", "tls", "tls_native", "handshake", "extensions", "ALPN", "alert", "decode_error", "illegal_parameter", "unsupported_extension", "ClientHello", "ServerHello", "compression_methods", "key_share", "RFC 8446", "RFC 7301", "RFC 5246", "D40", "D32", "D81", "D82", "D83", "CYRIUS-2026-0037"]
---
A server that passed against 6.7.13's native client can fail against 6.7.14's, with an alert — every one an RFC MUST on
peer input (LIB-8; no decision):
  - an extension type twice in a ServerHello / EncryptedExtensions block, TLS 1.2 or 1.3: illegal_parameter (the
    client took the first);
  - bytes after a 1.3 ServerHello's or EncryptedExtensions' extension block: decode_error (the check was `>`);
  - an ALPN answer that is not exactly ONE non-empty name filling the extension (RFC 7301 §3.1): decode_error, and
    nothing is stored (a list of two selected the first; a malformed body selected nothing and passed);
  - the 1.2 client's ServerHello is now walked against the 1.2 ClientHello it built: an unsolicited extension or an ALPN
    answer to no offer is unsupported_extension, a protocol not offered illegal_parameter, a session id over 32 bytes
    decode_error — and `tls_native_connect_12` SENDS the alert (it sent none).
The fix is on the server. A test that calls `tls_native_client_parse_server_hello_12` directly must build the client's
own hello on that ctx first (`tls_native_12_build_client_hello`), or every extension reads as unoffered.

6.7.15 — THE EXTENSION BLOCK FILLS ITS MESSAGE, BOTH WAYS (D40; PL-5, PL-6). A peer that completed a handshake against
6.7.14's native stack can fail against 6.7.15's, with decode_error:
  - a TLS 1.2 ServerHello with bytes after its extension block fails the native 1.2 client (`TLS_ERR_BAD_HANDSHAKE`,
    decode_error sent) — the 1.3 check came in 6.7.14;
  - the native SERVERS now read a client's ClientHello the same way: `tls_native_accept` refuses a 1.3 ClientHello with
    bytes after its block, and `tls_native_accept_12` / `tls_native_accept` a 1.2 one with bytes after the block, a
    block longer than the message (it negotiated, without EMS and ALPN) or one byte where the block's length goes.
A ClientHello with NO extension block still negotiates without extensions, and well-formed hellos (OpenSSL's, the
native stack's own) are unaffected. The fix is on the peer. The 1.2 server's other leniencies — a ClientHello with no
compression_methods, one overrunning the message, an extension overrunning its block — still negotiated in 6.7.15, and
its early refusals sent no alert; 6.7.16 closes both (below).

6.7.16 — THE NATIVE SERVERS READ THE WHOLE ClientHello (D32, D81 – D83; PL-9, PP-7 – PP-9). A hand-built, fuzzing or
broken client that negotiated against 6.7.15's native servers now meets the named alert instead of a ServerHello:
  - the 1.2 server (`tls_native_accept_12`, or `tls_native_accept` given a 1.2 hello): no compression_methods, an empty
    list or one overrunning the message, and an extension block that does not frame — decode_error (D32; an extension
    overrunning its block negotiated without EMS and ALPN, on the server's first group). A ClientHello too short for its
    random, session id or suites now receives its decode_error before the close (it got no alert);
  - the 1.3 server: an extension block ending in a 1 – 3 byte partial entry — decode_error (D81);
  - both: an extension type named twice — illegal_parameter (D82; they negotiated on the first copy);
    compression_methods without the null method (1.2) or anything but the single null byte (1.3) — illegal_parameter,
    and an empty 1.3 list decode_error (D83).
Well-formed hellos (OpenSSL's `s_client -tls1_2`, the native client's) are unchanged; the fix is on the peer. ⚠
CYRIUS-2026-0037 (P3, recorded in 6.7.16, not yet fixed): the 1.3 server checks each key_share against supported_groups
by a linear scan, so a peer's one 16 KB hello costs it ~24 ms of CPU before the refusal.
language/stdlib_modules.cyml tls_module_contract_v6614 (CONFORMANCE). CHANGELOG [6.7.14] LIB-8; [6.7.15] PL-5, PL-6;
[6.7.16] PL-9, PP-7 – PP-9.

[[entries]]
name = "a_corrupt_patra_value_reads_patra_err_page_not_an_empty_ok_v6715"
description = "Since patra 1.16.5 (folded in cyrius 6.7.15) a corrupt BYTES / TEXT value is an ERROR, not an empty value: the read answers `PATRA_ERR_PAGE` (7) with nothing written, and the length getter keeps the row's length — or answers -1 for a ref longer than the file (the user's decision, 2026-10-18). Through 1.16.4 the getter said 0 and the read `PATRA_OK`. Size a buffer only from a length >= 0, and check the read's return; -1 also means another column type or a cell outside the result. Since patra 1.16.6 (cyrius 6.7.16) a negative length is refused at the write (`PATRA_ERR_PARAM`) and a stored one reads -1 / `PATRA_ERR_PAGE` (D34, D35)."
category = "cyrius"
tags = ["v6.7.15", "v6.7.16", "patra", "1.16.5", "1.16.6", "fold", "BYTES", "TEXT", "PATRA_ERR_PAGE", "PATRA_ERR_PARAM", "PATRA_OK", "patra_insert_row", "patra_bind_text", "corruption", "file format", "database", "D34", "D35"]
---
    var n = patra_result_get_bytes_len(rs, row, col);     # 1.16.5: the row's length — or -1, no valid length
    if (n < 0) { … }                                       # -1: also another column type, or a cell outside the result
    var out = alloc(n + 1);
    if (patra_result_read_bytes(db, rs, row, col, out) != PATRA_OK) { … }   # PATRA_ERR_PAGE (7): corrupt, out untouched

WHAT CHANGED (patra 1.16.5; LF-2, LF-3, LF-7). A BYTES / TEXT cell whose stored chain is malformed — a bad page marker,
an oversized or negative page length, a link out of the file, a chain looping back, a ref shorter than its chain — read
back as an EMPTY value since patra 1.12.8: `patra_result_get_bytes_len` / `_get_text_len` said 0 and
`patra_result_read_bytes` / `_read_text` answered `PATRA_OK` with nothing written (a chain looping back read `PATRA_OK`
with the wrong bytes). Since 1.16.5 the getter keeps the row's length and the read answers `PATRA_ERR_PAGE`, nothing
written. A ref naming more bytes than the file can hold has NO valid length: the getter answers -1 and the read
`PATRA_ERR_PAGE` (since 1.14.0 it was 0 / `PATRA_OK`). A cell with a NEGATIVE stored length reads `PATRA_ERR_PAGE` too;
in 1.16.5 its getter answered the stored value, and `patra_insert_row` accepted a negative length (closed in 1.16.6,
below).

patra 1.16.6 (folded in cyrius 6.7.16; D34, D35, the user, 2026-10-19). A NEGATIVE LENGTH IS REFUSED AT THE WRITE:
`patra_insert_row` / `patra_insert_row_or_ignore` with a negative BYTES / TEXT length answer `PATRA_ERR_PARAM` (11) and
write nothing (they answered `PATRA_OK` and stored the length raw), and `patra_bind_text` with a negative length answers
`PATRA_ERR_PARAM` and stores nothing, the slot keeping what it held (an INSERT / UPDATE wrote the length, a WHERE value
matched nothing). A negative length ALREADY STORED — by an older patra or a damaged file — reads -1 from the getters and
`PATRA_ERR_PAGE` from the reads, as a ref longer than the file does. Check the insert's and the bind's return.

THE TRAPS. -1 is also the getters' answer for a column of another type and for a cell outside the result set — the READ
tells them apart (`PATRA_ERR_TYPE` / `PATRA_ERR_PARAM` there, `PATRA_ERR_PAGE` here). And code that sized a buffer from
the length and ignored the read's return now gets an UNTOUCHED buffer of that size where it got nothing: check the read.
Databases patra wrote are unaffected — only a corrupt or crafted file reads differently, on every target.
language/stdlib_modules.cyml patra_stdlib_module. CHANGELOG [6.7.15] LF-2, LF-3, LF-7 (patra 1.16.5); [6.7.16] W0-5
(patra 1.16.6).
